**Definition 9.1 (two physical boundary specimens).** Put

$$
\psi_T=\frac{(\sqrt{r_T},1,0)^T}{\sqrt{1+r_T}},\qquad
\psi_S=\frac{(\sqrt{r_S},0,1)^T}{\sqrt{1+r_S}} .
\tag{PG.42}
$$

They satisfy the off-plane quadratic of (PG.20). For the $T$ test let
$\eta=\alpha,\zeta=\beta$ and
$A=(h_0^2r_T+h_1^2)/(1+r_T)$.
For the $S$ test let
$\eta=\beta,\zeta=\alpha$ and
$A=(h_0^2r_S+h_2^2)/(1+r_S)$.
In either case define

$$
p=\frac{1-\zeta}{1+\eta},\quad q=\frac{\ell}{1+\eta},\quad
x=\sqrt p,\quad y=\sqrt q,\quad t=y/x .
\tag{PG.43}
$$

Thus $p+q=1$ and $p-\eta q=k$.

